# (round 112) In an OBSERVED program a module-level `for` over a PRE-BOUND
# module name wrote the module env slot -- the round-68 must-bind
# exemption encoded "a binder over an existing binding overwrites it",
# which the pinned VM refutes: the binder lives in a loop env, the module
# binding is untouched after the loop, and the module slot's observation
# history is untouched too (the in-loop rebind polluted it here: VM
# `equilibrium`, AOT `moving`). The traced regime already had the rule
# (t199, a C block-local); the observed regime cannot shadow env reads
# with a C local, so the binder is RENAMED to a fresh env name per loop
# (nested same-name loops get their own), which is what a child env is in
# a name-keyed world. Varied: numeric and string binders, pre-bound and
# never-bound, in-loop rebind, nested same-name loops, a post-loop write,
# and the module's verdicts after each.
